#header:
  #-: Zerolang Style Guide

#nav id=toc class=toc-sidebar:
  #div id=tocfile: Table of Contents
  #doc.toc

This document is the canonical style guide for Zerolang source code. It is
prescriptive: each rule states what to do, with a brief rationale only where
the rule is non-obvious. Status: the standard library, examples and
documentation will be migrated to comply with this guide in the next phase of
the project; existing source may diverge in places until that migration lands.


#--: General Formatting

#---: Encoding and File Extension

#ul:
  #*: Source files are UTF-8. This is mandated by the language.
  #*: Source files use the extension #~".z".
  #*: Files end with a single trailing newline. No trailing whitespace on any line.

#---: Indentation

#ul:
  #*: Indent with spaces. Tabs are not used.
  #*: One indent step is four spaces.

#---: Line Length

A soft limit of 100 columns. Wrap when a line would otherwise harm
readability; do not wrap purely to satisfy the limit when the result is worse.
Treat 100 as a guideline, not a hard fail. #~"zl lint --line-length N" reports
over-limit lines as advisories (#~"W005") that do not fail the gate.

#---: One Statement Per Line

Use one executable statement or expression per source line. Semicolons are
permitted by the grammar as line separators but should not appear in
production code -- line feeds are the natural terminator.

#---: Blank Lines

Separate definitions and logical blocks with a single blank line where it aids
readability. Do not stack blank lines: #~"zl fmt" caps a run at two between
definitions and at one inside a body, and requires one before a definition's
doc block and after a multi-line definition.

#---: Braces, Parentheses and Line Continuation

Inside code, parentheses suspend line-feed termination; braces reenable it.
Line continuation matters in expressions and statements; definitions filter
line feeds inside their value parse automatically and do not need this
treatment. The following rules cover wrapping in code:

#ul:
  [#*: An opening brace follows immediately after its related
  statement or expression on the same line.]
  [#*: When a statement continues after a closing brace, the continuation
  appears on the same line as that closing brace.]
  [#*: A braced or parenthesised clause that fits comfortably on a single
  line is written on a single line, with no whitespace inside the opening
  or closing delimiter.]
  [#*: A clause that does not fit on a single line places its contents on
  subsequent lines, indented one extra step (four spaces) deeper than the
  lines bearing the opening and closing delimiters. The closing delimiter
  sits on its own line at the parent indent.]
  [#*: When a logical line will not fit on one file line, use embedded
  parentheses or braces to wrap. If the expression has none, wrap the whole
  expression in parentheses so the multi-line construct reads as a single
  unit.]
  [#*: Within parenthesised expressions and braced clauses (including #~"in"
  parameter lists), introduce newlines wherever they improve readability --
  for example, one parameter per line.]

#code language=zerolang: """
  # single-line clause: no padding inside the parens
  result: (format value: 42 width: 6)

  # multi-line clause: contents indented one step beyond the delimiters
  result: (format
      value: 42
      width: 6
      pad: "0"
  )
  """

#---: Whitespace Around Identifiers

A single space separates identifiers that would otherwise abut and parse as a
single identifier. The numeric and comparison operators are ordinary
identifiers and #_+"must" be surrounded by whitespace.

#code language=zerolang: """
  total: a + b           # correct
  # totaL: a+b           # parses as a single identifier 'a+b'
  """

#---: Definitions and the Colon

Definitions are a label, then #~":", then an expression or definition. A
single space follows the colon when the definition continues on the same
line. A space #_+"before" the colon is forbidden by the language. #~"zl fmt"
writes that single space.

In a call argument, a label whose value is a variable of the same name
(#~"foo x: x") collapses to the #~":name" shorthand (#~"foo :x") — the two are
identical in meaning. #~"zl fmt" applies this (L006).

Do not horizontally align the right-hand sides of consecutive definitions or
their types. Aligned columns are noisy under diff and brittle under rename.

#code language=zerolang: """
  # correct
  host: String
  port: u16
  timeout: u32

  # avoid: padded for visual alignment
  # host:    String
  # port:    u16
  # timeout: u32
  """

#---: Parentheses for Precedence Only

Parentheses are used for precedence enforcement, for clarity in dense
expressions, and for the multi-line wrapping case above. Do not parenthesise
the conditions of #~"if" or #~"for", the subject of a single-line #~"match"
(see #> to=match-and-case: Match and Case), or the arguments of a top-level
call.

One exception applies when a #_+"top-level" call takes an argument that is
itself a call with named arguments: the argument call must be parenthesised
so it is evaluated before the outer call.

Likewise, an #~"if" or #~"for" condition that is #_+"itself" a call with named
arguments must be parenthesised, so its arguments do not run past the #~"then"
or #~"loop". #~"zl fmt" strips condition parentheses that are #_*"not" required
(e.g. around a comparison), leaving these, and puts them back when the
condition breaks across lines.

#code language=zerolang: """
  # bare top-level call: no parentheses needed
  print "hello"

  # nested named-arg call: inner call parenthesised
  print (format value: 42 width: 6)
  """

#---: Numeric Literals

Numeric literals may use underscores to group digits for readability:

#ul:
  #*: Decimal literals: groups of three from the right.
  #*: Binary and hexadecimal literals: groups of four. Use groups of eight only for literals 32 bits wide or larger.

Typed literals use the dot suffix form: #~"65.u8", not #~"65u8".

#code language=zerolang: """
  population: 7_900_000_000
  mask: 0xFFFF_FFFF
  marker: 0b1010_0101
  byte: 65.u8
  """


#---: Literal Type Inference

A typed suffix declares a literal's type at its #_*"origin". Most literals do
not need one: the type checker coerces a bare literal to the surrounding type
wherever the context fixes it -- an adjacent typed operand, the function return
type, the other side of a comparison, a typed parameter, or an assignment
target. Write the suffix only where no such context exists -- and for a zero or
empty initializer, write no literal at all: the bare type name
constructs the zero value (#~"seq: u64", #~"buf: String").
In a #_*"declaration" (parameter block or field list) the same
spellings divide differently: #~"width: u64" declares a required,
default-less slot, while #~"width: 0.u64" declares the type #_*"and"
the default -- choose by whether callers may omit the argument.

#code language=zerolang: """
  # context fixes the type -- leave the literal bare
  this.n = this.n + 1        # this.n is u64, so 1 is u64
  return 0                   # function is 'out u64', so 0 is u64
  if count < 2 then ...      # count is u64, so 2 is u64
  resize cap: 8              # parameter 'cap' is usize, so 8 is usize

  # zero origin -- the bare type name IS the zero value
  seq: u64                   # 0.u64; preferred over 'seq: 0.u64'
  buf: String                # empty owned string; preferred over '"".string'

  # genuine non-zero origin -- the suffix carries the type
  offset: 7.u64              # a bare '7' binding would default to i64
  total: 60.u32 * 60 * 24    # all-literal expression: one suffix anchors
                             # the whole expression (the bare operands
                             # coerce to u32); with none it folds to i64
  """

A bare binding (#~"x: 0") defaults to #~"i64", and an all-literal expression
with no typed operand folds to #~"i64". In those positions the suffix is
load-bearing, not noise. Inside a #~"data" block, prefer #~"out T" over
per-element suffixes -- see Data Blocks below.


#---: Data Blocks

When every element of a #~"data" block should share a type, use the
#~"out T" form. Per-element type tags (#~"name: 0.T") are redundant
when #~"out T" is present and prohibited in conflicting combinations.
Leave individual element values bare and let #~"out T" carry the type
for the whole block.

For data blocks whose elements are pure compile-time named constants
intended to coerce at each call site, omit #~"out" and let the literals
coerce per use.

#code language=zerolang: """
  # Preferred: out T carries the type, elements stay bare.
  bytemask: data {
      LOW:  0
      MID:  64
      HIGH: 250
  } out u8

  # Acceptable: untyped block when callers coerce per use site.
  primes: data { 2 3 5 7 11 }

  # Avoid: per-element type tags. The unification rule accepts this
  # (all u8), but the type repetition adds noise.
  bytemask_noisy: data { LOW: 0.u8 MID: 64.u8 HIGH: 250.u8 }
  """


#--: Match and Case

When the entire #~"match" fits comfortably on one line, write it on one
line and omit the parentheses around the subject. Use this form only when
every arm is a short statement.

#code language=zerolang: """
  match r case some then print "found" case none then print "missing"
  """

When the match spans multiple lines so each arm sits on its own line, place
the subject in parentheses on its own line. The parentheses let the subject
align with the case arms below it. Each #~"case" and the optional #~"else"
sits at the parent indent, immediately after the closing delimiter of the
previous arm.

#code language=zerolang: """
  match (
      result
  ) case ok then {
      print "value=\{result.value}"
  } case err then {
      print "failed: \{result.detail}"
  }
  """


#--: Empty Clauses

Do not write empty clause bodies to satisfy structure. They are noise and
usually signal that the control flow can be expressed more directly.

#ul:
  [#*: A statement #~"if" (its value is discarded) needs no #~"else". Drop an
  empty #~"else {}" entirely.]
  [#*: Never pair an empty #~"then {}" with a working #~"else". Negate the
  condition with #~"not" or #~"!=" and put the work in the #~"then" block.]
  [#*: A #~"match" on a union/variant stays exhaustive even as a statement, so
  a single required no-op arm is acceptable. Write a no-op arm as the bare
  #~"null" body — #~"else null", #~"case a then null" — never as empty braces
  (#~"zl fmt" writes those as #~"null"). When several arms are no-ops, collapse them into
  one #~"else null" rather than listing each.]

#code language=zerolang: """
  # avoid: empty else on a statement if
  if name == "i8" then { return "int8_t".string } else {}
  # prefer: drop the empty else
  if name == "i8" then return "int8_t".string

  # avoid: empty then, with the work in else
  if isC then {} else { return true }
  # prefer: negate the condition, work in then
  if not isC then return true

  # acceptable: a union match's one required no-op arm
  match (o) case some then { collect n: o } case none then {}
  """


#--: Naming

#---: Units

Unit names are lowercase printable ASCII with no whitespace; this is mandated
by the language for filesystem compatibility. In addition:

#ul:
  #*: Aim for a single word.
  #*: Do not use underscores.

#---: Valtype Definitions

#~"record", #~"variant" and #~"facet" definitions use a lowercase name. Aim
for a single word. If the name needs more than one word, run the words
together without underscores.

#code language=zerolang: """
  point: record { x: i64 y: i64 }
  filekind: variant {
      file: null
      dir: null
      symlink: null
      other: null
  }
  """

#---: Reftype Definitions

#~"class", #~"union" and #~"protocol" definitions use #_+"PascalCase". The
casing distinguishes reftypes from valtypes at the point of use. Compound
names split on word boundaries: #~"BufWriter", not #~"Bufwriter";
#~"ListView", not #~"Listview".

The predefined reftypes built into the language follow the same rule:
#~"String", #~"StringView", #~"Option", #~"OptionView", #~"Result",
#~"Text", and the generic bounds #~"AnyRef", #~"anyval",
#~"RefHashable", #~"valhashable".

#code language=zerolang: """
  File: class { ... }
  ListView: class { ... }
  ReadStream: protocol { ... }
  """

#---: Generic Parameters

A generic parameter names the type it stands for, so it is cased like a type:
#_+"its case matches the case of its bound". Every bound's own name already
follows the rule above, so this is a one-glance check with no exception.

#code language=zerolang: """
  t: anyval.generic
  T: AnyRef.generic
  K: RefHashable.generic
  k: valhashable.generic
  """

A bound that admits both families is cased by its own declaration kind, not by
what it admits: #~"StringLike" is a #~"union", so its parameter is #~"T".

Prefer very short names where readability is clear — #~"K" and #~"V" for a
map's key and value, #~"K" for a set's element, #~"T" for a single type
parameter. Keep a descriptive name where it carries meaning, cased by the same
rule: #~"From", #~"Gives".

This matters more here than in most languages. The family decides ownership
behaviour, so the case tells a reader whether a binding of that parameter
copies or moves, without scrolling back to the #~"as" clause to find out.

A value (non-type) parameter is bound by a numeric type, which is lowercase, so
it is lowercase too, and needs no carve-out:

#code language=zerolang: """
  to: u64.generic
  """

#---: Variant Tags

Variant arm names are values, not types, and are written lowercase. Aim for
a single word; if more is needed, use #~"lowerCamelCase".

#code language=zerolang: """
  filekind: variant {
      file: null
      dir: null
      symlink: null
      other: null
  }
  """

#---: Functions, Methods and Fields

Aim for a single lowercase word. If the name needs more than one word, use
#~"lowerCamelCase".

#ul:
  [#*: Getters list the name of the item being returned only. Do not prefix
  getters with #~"get".]
  [#*: Setters may be prefixed with #~"set" when the alternative would be
  ambiguous.]
  [#*: Predicate methods returning #~"bool" may be prefixed with #~"is" or
  #~"has".]

#code language=zerolang: """
  # correct
  f.size # not f.getSize
  user.setName name: "alice"
  conn.isOpen
  doc.hasErrors
  """

#---: Constants

Top-level value definitions use #~"lowerCamelCase". Do not use uppercase or
shouting snake case.

#code language=zerolang: """
  maxRetries: 3
  defaultPort: 8080
  """

#---: Prefer Full Words Over Contractions

Prefer full words over contractions for type, member, and value names.
Short forms are acceptable only when they match an established industry term
(e.g. #~"u8", #~"paren", #~"tab") or a one-letter scratch variable in a tight
scope. A two-letter saving is not worth an opaque reader experience.

#code language=zerolang: """
  # correct
  backspace: 8
  doubleQuote: 34
  stringBody: 0b0100

  # avoid
  # cBspace: 8        # bspace is an opaque contraction
  # cDquote: 34       # dquote is an opaque contraction
  # STRC: 0b0100      # interpreted-string-character contraction in shouting case
  """

ASCII control-byte mnemonics (#~"nul", #~"bs", #~"ht", #~"lf", #~"cr",
#~"del") are an exception: the two/three-letter forms are the ASCII-standard
names for those bytes.

#---: Receivers

The receiver in a method should be a short name -- preferably a single
letter -- that relates to the type. For multi-word reftype names, an
acronym is acceptable.

#ul:
  #*: All methods on the same type must use the same receiver name.
  #*: The #~":this" shorthand is permitted but a short type-relevant name is preferred.

#code language=zerolang: """
  File: class { ... } as {
      open: function {f: this name: StringView} out File is { ... }
      close: function {f: this} is { ... }
      size: function {f: this} out u64 is { ... }
  }

  ListView: class { ... } as {
      length: function {lv: this} out u64 is { ... }
      first: function {lv: this} out (Option T: of) is { ... }
  }
  """

#---: Platform and Architecture Identifiers

Platform and architecture identifiers may use their conventional external
spelling and are exempt from the no-underscores rule. Identifiability across
the wider ecosystem (Linux, Rust, Go, package metadata) outweighs the
in-language consistency cost.

#code language=zerolang: """
  archkind: variant {
      x86_64: null
      aarch64: null
      other: null
  }
  """


#--: Calls

When a function or method call has exactly one argument and that argument is
the leading parameter, omit the argument name.

#code language=zerolang: """
  print "hello"
  outbuf.append 65.u8
  """

Re-introduce the argument name when omitting it would obscure the call's
intent at the call site. The judgement applies most often to native or
generic methods where a bare argument's role is not obvious from the
receiver and method name.

#code language=zerolang: """
  # clear from receiver and method name -- omit
  names.append item

  # bare argument's role is not obvious -- name it
  buf.write from: chunk
  """

The same rule covers control-flow builtins whose clauses read like call
arguments. A #~"for" loop's leading condition is its first argument, named
#~"while"; omit that name when the condition leads. Keep #~"while" only on
the subsequent conditions of a multi-condition loop (they are not the first
argument).

#code language=zerolang: """
  # leading condition -- omit 'while'
  for i < n loop { ... }

  # subsequent conditions keep 'while'
  for i < n while ok loop { ... }
  """


#--: Item Definitions

When an item is generic, declare each generic parameter in the #~"as"
section as #~"name: <Constraint>.generic". Place the generic declarations
first in #~"as", before any conformances or static members. Instance
fields go in the #~"is" body, where they may reference the parameter
names directly.

#code language=zerolang: """
  Pair: class {
      first: a
      second: b
  } as {
      a: AnyRef.generic
      b: AnyRef.generic
  }
  """


#--: Functions

#---: Guard Clauses and Early Return

Use #~"if" guards with early return to handle errors and edge cases up
front. The remaining body is then the happy path with one less indent
level.

#code language=zerolang: """
  read: function {f: this} out (Result T: String) is {
      if not f.isOpen then return Result.err detail: "not open"
      if f.atEnd     then return Result.err detail: "eof"
      ...
      return Result.ok value: data
  }
  """

#---: Last-Expression Return

For short functions, return the last expression and omit the explicit
#~"return".

#code language=zerolang: """
  abs: function {n: i64} out i64 is {
      if n < 0 then 0 - n else n
  }
  """

#---: Expression Forms

Prefer #~"if" and #~"for" as expressions to produce values. Avoid
side-effecting an accumulator when an expression form is available.

#code language=zerolang: """
  # prefer
  squares: for x: 5.iterate loop { x * x }

  # avoid
  squares: (ListVal i64)
  for x: 5.iterate loop { squares.append x * x }
  """


#--: Unused Bindings

The linter warns on a local or parameter that is never read (L013 / L014)
and on a private definition or type member that is never referenced (L012).
Remove dead locals. When a binding must stay -- a parameter required by a
signature, a pedagogical example -- mark it as intentionally unused by
#i:"touching" it: write its name as a bare statement. The touch lowers to
#~"(void)(x);" in the emitted C, so it costs nothing at runtime.

#code language=zerolang: """
  onEvent: function {kind: u64 payload: u64} is {
      payload  # touch: required by the handler signature, not used here
      dispatch :kind
  }
  """

A leading underscore does #b:"not" mean "ignored": #~"_dist" is a private
name like any other, and an unused #~"_"-prefixed binding still warns. The
bare-statement touch is the only sanctioned opt-out.

#--: Ownership Annotations

Ownership annotations behave differently in parameter declarations and in
inline code. The two are covered separately below.

#---: Parameter Declarations

#ul:
  [#*: #~".borrow" is the default for #~"in" parameters. Do not write
  #~".borrow" on an #~"in" parameter.]
  [#*: #~".take" is the default for #~"out" and #~"outx" types. Do not
  write #~".take" on a return type.]
  [#*: #~"outx T.borrow" is the only place #~".borrow" is written
  explicitly. It indicates a reference return, which is always #~"outx":
  the reference points into a non-owned #~"in" parameter -- #~".view"
  (readonly) or #~".borrow" (mutable) -- or the receiver, and #~"outx"
  keeps that parent locked for the result's lifetime.]
  [#*: #~".view" and #~".hold" have no default. Write them explicitly when
  a readonly receiver or binding is intended -- the stdlib's readers
  (#~"length", #~"iterate", ...) declare #~"t: this.view".]
  [#*: #~"outx" is the keyword, not a marker: write it when the result
  must keep the call's inputs locked, and #~"out" everywhere else. It is
  required when an owned result's type carries a non-owned field (the
  wrapper-constructor shape: #~"outx this"), and refused when nothing is
  there to pin.]

#code language=zerolang: """
  # in: borrow is implicit; out: take is implicit
  area: function {p: point} out f64 is { ... }

  # reference return: outx, explicit .borrow on the type, a non-owned parent
  first: function as { t: AnyRef.generic } in {l: (List t).borrow} outx t.borrow is { ... }
  """

#---: Inline Code

#ul:
  [#*: Do not write #~".borrow" in function bodies or on call arguments.
  Borrow is implicit at every position where it is the default.]
  [#*: Use #~".take" only when there is a receiving variable or parameter
  of that type. The compiler enforces #~".take" based on the receiving
  parameter; the call-site annotation is rarely needed for safety.]
  [#*: As a general rule, omit #~".take" on call arguments. Add it only
  where a specific call's readability is meaningfully improved.]
  [#*: #~".take" may be used to move a reftype to a new variable name when
  the new name improves documentation or clarity. Do not rename for its
  own sake.]
  [#*: #~".take" as a bare expression to drop a reftype is forbidden.
  Use #~".drop".]
  [#*: #~".drop" is a standalone statement. It returns null and may
  not appear inside an assignment, expression, or call argument.]

#---: Borrow and Drop Methods

#ul:
  [#*: #~".borrow" is permitted as a borrow-projection on a reftype variable
  to produce a borrowed reference for the duration of an expression or
  protocol creation.]
  [#*: #~".drop" on a borrowed variable, written as a standalone
  statement, ends the borrow.]

#code language=zerolang: """
  borrowed: provider.borrow from: m2.borrow
  borrowed.use
  borrowed.drop
  print "m2 still alive: factor=\{m2.factor}"
  """


#--: Comments and Documentation

#---: Line Comments

Zerolang line comments begin with #~"#". Comments document #_+"why", not
what; do not paraphrase code that names itself. Place comments on the line
above the item they describe, not trailing the code.

#_*"The bar for any comment is that it adds something the name and the type
cannot." A doc that restates its declaration is worse than none: it costs a
reader the time to read it and teaches the eye to skip #~"#" blocks, which is
how the one comment that mattered gets skipped too. If nothing can be added,
that is a signal to rename the thing rather than to write filler.

Inside a body, default to writing no comment at all. A statement-level comment
earns its place only where the reader needs context the code cannot give:
a non-obvious invariant, a lock ordering, the failure a guard exists to
prevent. State such a thing #_+"once" — an invariant repeated at every site it
holds is one invariant and N-1 copies, and #b:"L025" reports it.

#---: What zls Reads

Every declaration doc reaches the reader through #~"textDocument/hover", so
the forms below are not conventions but the shapes the language server
actually looks for:

#ul:
  [#*: a #~"#" block on the lines directly above a definition]
  [#*: a #~"#" block above a parameter that starts its own source line]
  [#*: a trailing #~"#" on a parameter's or a field's own line]
  [#*: the block at the top of a file, which documents the unit]

#_*"A doc block must sit flush against its declaration." The reader stops at
the first blank line, so a blank between the block and what it describes
silently detaches it.

#---: Declaration Docs

The first line of a definition's block is the declared identifier, two dashes,
and a one-sentence gloss; further lines carry the contract a caller cannot see
— ownership, locking, failure modes.

#code language=zerolang: """
  # env -- look up environment variable `key`. Returns Option.none if
  # the variable is not set; Option.some with the value otherwise.
  env: function {key: StringView} out (Option T: String) is native
  """

A #_+"field or arm" is documented when its name and type do not say enough —
the units, the sentinel's meaning, the invariant, who writes it. When they do,
leave it bare: a line that restates the declaration is filler, and a count of
documented members is not a measure of anything. The doc, when there is one,
takes one line above the declaration, flush against it: a lowercase noun
phrase, no #~"name --" prefix, no trailing period.

#code language=zerolang: """
  # the svalKind payload pool; cval.strId indexes it 1-based (0 = none)
  # the root of the path a borrowed variable borrows from (0 when none)
  """

A #_+"parameter" is documented on the same terms, one line above it, and the
parameter must start its own source line to carry one. Explode a single-line
parameter list to multi-line only when at least one parameter needs
explaining; otherwise cover the parameters in the function's own block. Note
that #~"zl fmt" explodes a list for #_+"width" as well, and a list exploded
that way is not a request for documentation.

#---: Do Not Duplicate the .pdoc Pages

#~"docs/compiler.pdoc" owns the pipeline and the cross-cutting semantic rules;
#~"docs/typechecker.pdoc" owns the core data structures. A source comment
carries the #_+"local" invariant and the failure the code guards against, not a
restatement of either page. Where a back-link helps, cite the document and
section #_*"by name — never by line number", which rots.

#---: The Rules That Enforce This

#ul:
  [#*: #b:"L016" #b:"L017" #b:"L018" report an undocumented definition,
    parameter or member. All three are opt-in: #~"zl lint --docs --docs-params
    --docs-fields". L017 and L018 are advisory lists for a reader, not gates:
    whether a member or parameter needs a doc is a judgment no count can make.]
  [#*: #b:"--docs-private" measures a unit's whole surface rather than only the
    names its #~"public:" block exports. Without it a unit that exports a
    handful of names reports almost nothing.]
  [#*: #b:"L024" reports a doc whose every content word already appears in the
    declaration below it, and #b:"L025" a doc body repeated more than twice in
    one file — compared with the declaration's own words and every width folded
    out, so a body written once per type is the one body it is. Both are opt-in
    via #~"--docs-quality"; #~"--docs-all" turns on every doc flag at once.]
  [#*: the corpus runner ratchets L016, L024 and L025 against
    #~"tests/fixtures/doc_baseline.txt" under #~"make ci". A count may only go
    down, and the target is zero for every row.]

#--: Errors and Panics

#~"error msg: \"...\"" is the compile-time form; #~"panic msg: \"...\"" is
its runtime counterpart. Apply the same phrasing rules to both:

#ul:
  #*: Lowercase first letter.
  #*: No trailing punctuation.
  #*: Present tense, declarative.
  #*: State the failed condition, not a stack-trace narrative.

#code language=zerolang: """
  if size > limit then error msg: "buffer exceeds maximum capacity"

  if conn.closed then panic msg: "write on closed connection"
  """


#--: File Hygiene

#ul:
  #*: One trailing newline at end of file.
  #*: No trailing whitespace on any line.
  #*: A unit declaration, when present, sits at the very top of the file, before any imports or item definitions.
  #*: A unit's #~"public:" interface block, when present, sits at the top of the file -- immediately after the header comment and before any definition. It declares the names other units may reference; everything else is private. Enforced by #b:"L019".
